1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,420
How can Articulate continuously
evaluate an architecture rather than

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:06,430
simply store it?

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,400
That's the question for this episode.

4
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:12,990
Hello, I'm Russell, and
welcome to episode 15 of

5
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:16,420
the Articulate Journal, where
I'm exploring what it takes

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:19,830
to build an AI-native architecture
from first principles.

7
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:24,130
In the previous episode, I explored
how architectural knowledge

8
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:29,300
evolves. New information simply doesn't
overwrite what Articulate already

9
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:32,800
knows. It enters as proposed knowledge.

10
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:37,200
It's interpreted, reconciled,
and assessed before it can become

11
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:39,600
part of the authoritative Knowledge Model.

12
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,300
That gives us a way of deciding what we

13
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:45,810
currently believe to be
true about the architecture.

14
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:47,870
But there's another problem.

15
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,640
Just because something is
true doesn't mean it's good

16
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:55,420
architecture. Having what an
architecture looks like is very

17
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:58,860
different from evaluating
what the architecture means.

18
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:03,870
Imagine Articulate knows that a
reporting service reads directly

19
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:05,560
from the Order database.

20
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:07,900
That might be completely correct.

21
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:09,830
We have evidence for it.

22
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:13,340
We're confident it's happening
and it belongs in the

23
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:14,800
architecture model.

24
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:17,510
But suppose we have an
architecture principle saying a

25
00:01:17,590 --> 00:01:21,560
service should have exclusive
ownership of its persistence.

26
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,870
Now we have two pieces of
architectural knowledge.

27
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:28,120
The reporting service reads
from the Order database.

28
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:32,790
And the Order Service should
exclusively own that database.

29
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:36,830
Neither statement explicitly
says there's a problem.

30
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,840
The concern only appears when
we evaluate them together.

31
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,870
The architecture may work.

32
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:44,870
The knowledge may be correct.

33
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:47,870
But there's potentially
an architectural issue.

34
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:50,840
And that's a very
different kind of problem.

35
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,800
This is much closer to what
architects actually do.

36
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:58,770
We're rarely just looking for information.

37
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,790
We're constantly evaluating it.

38
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:04,900
This design conflicts with
one of our other principles.

39
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:08,800
Does the proposal undermine
a previous decision?

40
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:13,080
Does this improve reliability
but make the system harder

41
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,040
to operate?

42
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:15,860
And do we accept that trade-off?

43
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:19,270
Are we introducing a new
dependency on something that

44
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:20,790
we're trying to retire?

45
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:23,850
Is there something important missing?

46
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:26,430
We've taken what we know
and we're asking what

47
00:02:26,510 --> 00:02:29,040
it means in a context of
the wider architecture.

48
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:31,680
And this takes me back to something I said

49
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:34,800
when I first introduced
the Knowledge Model.

50
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:37,890
Knowledge is information
combined with thinking.

51
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:40,340
Up until now, I've spent a lot of time

52
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:43,820
establishing the information
side of that statement.

53
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:49,760
Architectural assurance starts
to make thinking executable.

54
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:51,880
Consider another example.

55
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:55,790
Suppose we're proposing
a new loyalty service.

56
00:02:56,170 --> 00:02:59,760
As part of the design,
that service depends on

57
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:01,810
an existing CRM platform.

58
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:04,500
If we look at the current
architecture, there may

59
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:06,800
be absolutely nothing wrong with that.

60
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:12,140
The CRM exists, it's operational,
other systems already depend

61
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:12,820
upon it.

62
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:15,830
The relationship is perfectly valid.

63
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:18,560
But elsewhere in a
Knowledge Model, we have an

64
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:20,790
accepted architectural decision.

65
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:22,850
The CRM is going to be retired.

66
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:27,630
Again, none of the individual
pieces of knowledge explicitly

67
00:03:27,710 --> 00:03:31,840
say this proposal introduces
an architectural risk.

68
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:33,830
We have to connect them.

69
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,670
The loyalty service introduces
a new dependency on a

70
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:40,120
CRM and that CRM is
scheduled for retirement.

71
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:44,420
Therefore, this proposal increases
our dependency on something we're

72
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:46,560
deliberately trying to remove.

73
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,480
That conclusion wasn't stored
in a Knowledge Model, it

74
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:51,800
was derived from it.

75
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,840
This also reinforces something
from the previous episode.

76
00:03:56,540 --> 00:03:58,900
Understanding the baseline
architecture matters.

77
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:02,820
Architecture isn't only
about what exists today.

78
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:06,920
We need to understand
what once existed and what

79
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:10,650
exists now and what
we're intending to build.

80
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:14,180
A proposal can therefore be
completely valid against today's

81
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:17,860
architecture, while moving us
in exactly the wrong direction.

82
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:22,780
That's something a simple
validation rule can easily miss.

83
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:25,870
The question isn't, is
this relationship valid?

84
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:30,870
It's also, does this relationship
make sense given where

85
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:33,680
the architecture is trying to go?

86
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:36,810
That's architectural assessment.

87
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,780
This is the responsibility that I'm
calling Architectural Assurance.

88
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:47,530
Architectural Assurance evaluates
architectural knowledge and proposed

89
00:04:47,610 --> 00:04:50,860
change against the wider
architectural context.

90
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,460
That can happen at several levels.

91
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:55,790
Some concerns are structural.

92
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:58,870
A service depends on something
that no longer exists.

93
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:02,920
A component has no owner
or a business capability

94
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:05,550
has no supporting application.

95
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:08,820
Some concerns relate to principles.

96
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:12,180
The design works but
appears to conflict with an

97
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:14,040
architectural principle.

98
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,840
And some concerns are about trade-offs.

99
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:21,800
A proposed change may improve
resilience but increase operational

100
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:27,770
complexity. It might improve
availability, while also increasing costs.

101
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:31,870
Architecture isn't about
maximizing every characteristic.

102
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:35,920
It's about understanding the
consequences of the choices that

103
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:36,790
we're making.

104
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:41,790
Architectural Assurance should help
us make those consequences visible.

105
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,870
There's another important
distinction here.

106
00:05:45,150 --> 00:05:47,460
Not every assurance check
needs to go through a

107
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:48,850
large language model.

108
00:05:49,130 --> 00:05:52,330
If a component depends
on a system that doesn't

109
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:55,560
exist, we can determine that reliably.

110
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:59,180
If a service accesses a
database owned by another

111
00:05:59,190 --> 00:06:03,180
service, the Knowledge Model may
already contain enough structured

112
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:05,890
information to identify that relationship.

113
00:06:06,130 --> 00:06:09,450
If a proposal introduces
a dependency on a system

114
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:12,750
scheduled for retirement,
we may be able to discover

115
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:16,790
that through structured
queries and relationships.

116
00:06:17,090 --> 00:06:20,290
We shouldn't ask an LLM to
rediscover something the

117
00:06:20,370 --> 00:06:23,120
system can establish deterministically.

118
00:06:23,190 --> 00:06:27,520
But identifying a relationship and
deciding what that relationship

119
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:29,560
means are different problems.

120
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:33,230
Does the principle genuinely
apply here and is the

121
00:06:33,310 --> 00:06:34,800
violation justified?

122
00:06:35,130 --> 00:06:38,800
Is this an acceptable
transitional architecture?

123
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:43,050
Does the improvement in resilience
justify the additional operational

124
00:06:43,130 --> 00:06:46,840
complexity? Do we have
enough evidence to make that

125
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:51,120
judgement? That's where contextual
reasoning becomes useful.

126
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:56,100
So the architecture needs both
deterministic mechanisms, establish what

127
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:58,120
we can identify reliably.

128
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:03,720
And contextual reasoning helps
interpret why it matters.

129
00:07:04,190 --> 00:07:07,060
And this doesn't have to
begin once we've written

130
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:11,160
software. Earlier in the
journal, I introduced DCL as

131
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:15,920
a way of describing capability intent
independently of implementation.

132
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:19,200
If that intent is structured,
we can evaluate it

133
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:19,840
as well.

134
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:23,920
A capability might define what
happens when something succeeds,

135
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:27,800
but say nothing about
insufficient evidence.

136
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:30,820
Two rules might contradict each other.

137
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:35,440
An effect might modify authoritative
knowledge without defining an

138
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:36,820
approval policy.

139
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:40,840
A capability might consume an
event that nobody produces.

140
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:45,590
Those are architectural concerns
we can identify before we've

141
00:07:45,670 --> 00:07:48,420
chosen a framework or
create a service or written

142
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:48,940
a prompt.

143
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:55,120
So Architectural Assurance isn't about
evaluating implemented architecture.

144
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:58,120
It can also evaluate architectural intent.

145
00:07:58,140 --> 00:08:01,920
And finding those problems before
implementation is considerably cheaper

146
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:03,920
than discovering them afterwards.

147
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:06,830
But there's an important boundary here.

148
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:09,870
Architectural Assurance produces findings.

149
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:13,780
It does not produce unquestionable truth.

150
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:18,210
Suppose Articulate says this
proposal appears to conflict with

151
00:08:18,290 --> 00:08:22,850
the principle that services
exclusively own their own persistence.

152
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:27,050
That finding might be
correct, but there may be

153
00:08:27,130 --> 00:08:28,830
an approved exception.

154
00:08:29,130 --> 00:08:31,900
The relationship might have
been classified indirectly.

155
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:35,920
The principle might not apply
in this particular context.

156
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:39,850
Or the architecture might
deliberately accept that trade-off.

157
00:08:40,170 --> 00:08:42,900
The role of architectural
assurance is not to make

158
00:08:42,980 --> 00:08:43,890
that decision.

159
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:45,900
It's to make that decision visible.

160
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:48,830
A finding might lead to more discovery.

161
00:08:49,090 --> 00:08:50,880
It might lead to a revised design.

162
00:08:51,090 --> 00:08:52,880
It might result in an exception.

163
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:55,890
And it might trigger a new
architectural decision.

164
00:08:56,170 --> 00:08:59,850
And eventually, it might result
in architectural knowledge.

165
00:09:00,170 --> 00:09:03,870
But reasoning shouldn't silently
refine architectural truth.

166
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:09,780
Human architects still need to
provide judgment and accountability.

167
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:13,920
This creates the possibility of
something I've wanted Articulate

168
00:09:14,030 --> 00:09:15,820
to support from the beginning.

169
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:18,860
Continuous architectural assessment.

170
00:09:19,120 --> 00:09:22,570
As new knowledge enters
the system, we can evaluate

171
00:09:22,650 --> 00:09:23,790
what it implies.

172
00:09:24,130 --> 00:09:27,500
As proposals are created,
we can assess them against

173
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:28,800
what we already know.

174
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:34,040
As decisions change, we can
understand what those changes

175
00:09:34,050 --> 00:09:38,420
affect. As capabilities are
defined, we can identify gaps

176
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:40,830
before implementation begins.

177
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:44,920
Architecture starts becoming something
the system can actively reason

178
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,860
about, rather than something we
periodically document and review.

179
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:50,880
But there's a danger here as well.

180
00:09:51,110 --> 00:09:54,340
If every change produces
dozens of warnings, may one

181
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:55,800
will pay attention to them.

182
00:09:56,530 --> 00:10:00,570
Architectural Assurance shouldn't become
another governance gate that teams

183
00:10:00,650 --> 00:10:01,860
learn to work around.

184
00:10:02,150 --> 00:10:04,420
The objective isn't to
find as many problems as

185
00:10:04,450 --> 00:10:07,500
possible. It's to surface the
concerns that materially affect

186
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:08,880
architectural outcomes.

187
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:13,750
Assurance should support
judgment, not replace it.

188
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:17,700
And definitely not overwhelm it.

189
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:22,510
We've spent 15 episodes working
through the conceptual architecture

190
00:10:22,550 --> 00:10:23,810
behind Articulate.

191
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:25,900
We've defined the Knowledge Model.

192
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:29,180
We've explored how agents
can retrieve and reason over

193
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:30,800
architectural knowledge.

194
00:10:31,150 --> 00:10:34,790
We've used DCL to describe
capability intent.

195
00:10:35,070 --> 00:10:37,900
We've established the requirements
for an AI-native runtime.

196
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:40,850
We've explored memory
and durable execution.

197
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:45,120
We've selected that runtime, and
we've established how architectural

198
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:46,860
knowledge safely evolves.

199
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:50,940
And now we've added
Architectural Assurance, a way of

200
00:10:51,030 --> 00:10:53,560
evaluating what knowledge implies.

201
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:57,780
But so far, all of this
is still architecture.

202
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:00,890
Models, capabilities,
decisions and principles.

203
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:05,790
Ideas about how architectural
intelligence should work.

204
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:09,850
Eventually, architecture has
to confront implementation.

205
00:11:10,090 --> 00:11:12,200
So the question for the next episode is a

206
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:12,880
simple one.

207
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:15,900
Does this architecture
actually work when we build it?

208
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:19,180
See you on the next one!
